links:
  # Servo motor itself
  - part: servo/mg90s
    name: motor

  # Mounting screws
  - part: fastener/socketheadcap-iso4762
    package: //pub/std/metric/cqwarehouse
    name: screw-top
    params:
      size: M2.5-0.45
      length: 10
    connect:
      name: motor
      to: "//pub/std/metric/m:m2.5-thru-2.5"
      toInstance: top-front
  - part: fastener/socketheadcap-iso4762
    package: //pub/std/metric/cqwarehouse
    name: screw-bottom
    params:
      size: M2.5-0.45
      length: 10
    connect:
      name: motor
      to: "//pub/std/metric/m:m2.5-thru-2.5"
      toInstance: bottom-front

  # Servo shoulder
  - part: servo/shoulder-unidir
    name: shoulder
    connect:
      withInstance: inner
      name: motor
      to: "//pub/std/metric/m:m2.5-threaded-hole-4"
      comment: >-
        Pushed onto the splined output boss. An interference fit is what holds
        a servo horn on, so the two share the material the splines bite into -
        which a STEP model carries as solid, having no splines to deform.
      how:
        snapIn: true
  - part: fastener/countersunk-iso7046
    package: //pub/std/metric/cqwarehouse
    name: screw-horn
    params:
      size: M2.5-0.45
      length: 3.5
    connect:
      withParams:
        moveZ: 0.35
      name: shoulder
      toInstance: outer
      comment: >-
        The countersunk head beds into the horn, whose hole is modelled as a
        plain 1mm through hole with no countersink to receive the cone. Pushed
        home rather than seated on a matching taper, so the two share that much
        space.
      # It is connected to the horn and carries on into the motor behind it,
      # which nothing about the connection itself can say.
      interferes: motor
      how:
        snapIn: true
